Findings (Repo-Wide, Ordered by Severity)
Broken auth middleware import crashes protected backend routes
backend/Routes/Students/student.routes.js:2 imports verifyGoogleToken, but backend/middlewares/auth.middleware.js:50 exports verifyJWT.
Same mismatch appears in other protected route files (Admin, Admission_Cell, General_Office, Account_Office, Hod).
Impact: protected route modules fail at startup/import time.

Cookie security is unsafe for production
backend/utils/cookie.utils.js:3 sets secure: false.
Impact: auth cookie can travel over HTTP if deployed behind misconfigured TLS, increasing token theft risk.

Payment submission trusts client transaction data without external verification
backend/Controllers/student.controller.js:114 accepts transactionId / amount and persists them (backend/Controllers/student.controller.js:133) without gateway verification.
Impact: payment fraud risk (forged transaction IDs).

Payment + application status updates are non-atomic
backend/Controllers/student.controller.js:130 updates payment, then backend/Controllers/student.controller.js:148 updates application status in a separate write without transaction/session.
Impact: partial failures can leave inconsistent admission/payment state.

Document upload endpoint lacks strong URL/file validation
backend/Controllers/student.controller.js:62 accepts fileUrl, fileName, mimeType with minimal checks.
Impact: unsafe URL storage, weak file integrity checks, future SSRF risk if URLs are fetched server-side.

Hardcoded backend API URL in frontend login flow
frontend/src/app/login/page.tsx:18 posts to http://localhost:8080/api/auth/google.
Impact: environment portability/release risk and brittle deployments.

Frontend auth/session flow is incomplete
frontend/src/app/login/page.tsx:17 logs in but does not manage authenticated app state or redirect/guard logic; admission pages are directly renderable (frontend/src/app/admission/page.tsx:1).
Impact: UX and access-control assumptions depend entirely on backend responses; weak client-side session behavior.

Use of any in auth callback weakens type safety
frontend/src/app/login/page.tsx:13 uses credentialResponse: any.
Impact: runtime-safe handling of OAuth payload is not enforced by TS.

Form draft recovery trusts raw localStorage JSON shape
frontend/src/context/AdmissionContext.tsx:75 does JSON.parse(savedDraft) and directly sets state.
Impact: malformed/stale payloads can corrupt form state; no schema guard at boundary.

No automated tests configured across repo
backend/package.json:8 has placeholder failing test script; no *.test.* / *.spec.* files found in workspace.
Impact: high regression risk for auth/admission/payment workflows.

Open Questions / Assumptions
Assumed verifyGoogleToken is intended alias for verifyJWT; if not, a separate Google-token middleware may be missing entirely.
Assumed payment integrity should be server-verified against an external source; if this is intentionally manual/offline, that process needs explicit audit fields and operator workflow controls.
Assumed frontend route guarding is desired for UX; backend RBAC must remain the true authority regardless.
